- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid unsafe C input functions that can overflow buffers
WHAT: Using gets() or any input function that does not limit the number of characters read into a fixed-size buffer can cause buffer overflows when input exceeds the buffer capacity.
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, crash the program, or allow arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Never use gets(); instead, use fgets() with the destination buffer size, then remove the trailing newline if present and flush any remaining input from stdin.
EXAMPLE: Noncompliant: `char buf[1024]; gets(buf);`
EXAMPLE: Compliant: `char buf[1024]; if (fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin)) { char *p = strchr(buf, '\n'); if (p) *p = '\0'; }`

- Guarantee that library functions do not form invalid pointers or read/copy beyond valid object bounds
WHAT: Failure to validate attacker-controlled length fields before using them in pointer arithmetic or library calls (for example, memcpy) can cause reads or writes past the end of a buffer, forming invalid pointers and exposing out-of-bounds memory.
WHY: Out-of-bounds access can leak sensitive data (as in Heartbleed), corrupt memory, or cause crashes, enabling information disclosure or remote code execution.
HOW: For any length or offset derived from untrusted input, first check that all computed indexes and sizes (header + length + padding, etc.) are strictly within the actual buffer/record length before using them in pointer arithmetic, allocation sizes, or library calls; reject or truncate requests that exceed bounds.
EXAMPLE: Before: `memcpy(dst, src + header_len, payload);` with `payload` taken directly from the client. After: `if (header_len + payload <= buf_len) memcpy(dst, src + header_len, payload); else reject_request();`

- Verify that key material is not exposed to the application but instead uses an isolated security module like a vault for cryptographic operations.

- Use secure random number generators for security-sensitive operations
WHAT: Using weak PRNGs (for example, rand(), random(), drand48()) for IDs, keys, or tokens makes outputs predictable and guessable.
WHY: Predictable random values can let attackers forge IDs, bypass authentication, or recover cryptographic keys.
HOW: For non-cryptographic needs, use higher-quality PRNGs provided by the platform (for example, random(), rand48, arc4random()) and seed them properly; for cryptographic purposes, use OS- or hardware-backed CSPRNGs (for example, /dev/random, hardware RNGs, or CryptGenRandom() on Windows).
EXAMPLE: Before (non-compliant, C): `int id = rand();` used for a session token; After (compliant, Windows C): `BYTE buf[16]; CryptGenRandom(hProv, sizeof(buf), buf);` used to generate the token.

- MEM31-C: Free dynamically allocated memory when no longer needed
WHAT: Failing to call free() on dynamically allocated memory (e.g., from malloc()) before the lifetime of the last pointer to it ends causes memory to remain permanently allocated (memory leak).
WHY: Memory leaks can exhaust available memory, leading to degraded performance, denial of service, or process termination over time.
HOW: For every successful call to a standard allocation function (malloc, calloc, realloc), ensure a corresponding free() is invoked on the same pointer before all references to it go out of scope or are overwritten; structure code so every exit path (including error paths) frees owned allocations.
EXAMPLE:
Noncompliant: `char *buf = malloc(N); if (!buf) return -1; return 0;`
Compliant: `char *buf = malloc(N); if (!buf) return -1; /* use buf */ free(buf); return 0;`

- Do not access freed memory
WHAT: Accessing memory after it has been freed (including using stale pointers after realloc) can lead to use-after-free or double-free conditions.
WHY: Use-after-free and double-free bugs can cause crashes, data corruption, or be exploited for arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Immediately update all pointers after realloc, set freed pointers to NULL, and ensure no code path uses a pointer after free; avoid freeing the same allocation more than once.
EXAMPLE (C):
// Before: stale pointer after realloc
p = realloc(p, new_size); use_old_alias(p_alias); // p_alias still points to old memory
// After: update all aliases and nullify on free
tmp = realloc(p, new_size); if (!tmp) handle_error(); p = tmp; p_alias = p; free(p); p = p_alias = NULL;



